  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender equality</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender inequality in Fiji remains an ongoing challenge. Fiji has high rates of gender-based violence, with 64 per cent of women reporting physical or sexual abuse in their lifetime. In 2023, the Australian Government supported the provision of gender-based violence services in Fiji. These services were delivered mainly by the Fiji Women&#x2019;s Crisis Centre (FWCC), and Women&#x2019;s Fund Fiji. Counselling, advocacy and support for victim-survivors appear to have contributed to increase in access to justice; behavioral change; and progress towards changes in policy, protocols and procedures. FWCC also continued to respond to referrals for rape and sexual assault survivors, and provided support to victim-survivors through the National Domestic Violence Toll-free Helpline.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender equality</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender inequality is an ongoing challenge in Fiji, and  rates of Gender-Based Violence (GBV) remain alarmingly high. 64 percent of women in Fiji report experiencing physical or sexual abuse in their lifetime. In 2024, the Australian Government provided AUD 4.5 million to deliver 14,273 GBV services across Fiji. These crucial services were primarily delivered by the Fiji Women&#x2019;s Crisis Centre (FWCC) and Women&#x2019;s Fund Fiji (WFF). Counselling, advocacy, and support for victim-survivors has  contributed to increased access to justice, behavioural change, and strengthened  policy, protocols, and procedures. In 2024, the FWCC also  responded to referrals for rape and sexual assault survivors and provided support to victim-survivors through the National Domestic Violence Toll-free Helpline.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
